TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction (Number of spacecraft)"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction (Users)"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch / Table 2"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit / Table 4"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit (Manoeuvres)"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications / Table 5"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3–2.5, Appendix A"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Figures 1–4, Table 5, Appendix A"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Contents, Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Table 1 / 2.3 Orbit","issue":"5 m GSD at 600 km implies a focal length of roughly 0.6–1.2 m (5–10 µm pixels); fitting this in a 0.6 m box requires folded optics and is not discussed, though the diffraction aperture check is correct."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"No X-band link budget (antenna gain, EIRP, C/N0, modulation) to substantiate 50 Mbit/s to a 12 m dish; acknowledged by the author as further work."},{"where":"2.4 / Table 5","issue":"STK access computed at 0° elevation with no constraint, then corrected approximately by hand to 10° (60% usable); an elevation constraint in STK would be more rigorous."},{"where":"Table 3","issue":"Revisit estimate uses equatorial ground-track spacing and a nadir strip model; latitude-dependent spacing and roll-access geometry would change the ~5 day figure somewhat."},{"where":"2.1 / 2.5","issue":"No mass or power budget (solar array size, battery capacity for the 34 min eclipse) to demonstrate the 50 kg bus closes."},{"where":"Table 7","issue":"Air density at 600 km (1e-13 kg/m³) is highly variable with solar activity; the lifetime figures rest on a single value, though the uncertainty is acknowledged."}],"overall":"A strong, technically sound preliminary design: launch trade, verified J2 sun-synchronous orbit, STK access work, data budget and drag-sail EOL all check out numerically and are well evidenced. Main gaps are the missing link/power/mass budgets and the unexamined optics volume, but these are largely acknowledged. Roughly 88%.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"All four required elements (primary mission, rationale/need, number of spacecraft with justification, operator and customers) are addressed in detail with literature support on bleaching monitoring and resolution limitations. Well argued and specific; minor reliance on non-scholarly sources (National Geographic) keeps it just below top.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used. This also simplifies communications, reduces cost"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS) ... The customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":54.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Launch site/vehicle with trade and fallback, orbit type and full elements, manoeuvre rationale, ground station, contact frequency from STK access and data budget, duration and EOL disposal are all covered. Hand calculations (period, J2 SSO inclination, swath, track spacing, drag decay, data budget) check out numerically and are validated against STK. Gaps: no link budget, no power/mass budget, optics focal-length/volume feasibility unexamined.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"cos i = −(1.991 × 10⁻⁷) / (1.5 × 1.0826 × 10⁻³ × 0.8354 × 1.0831 × 10⁻³) = −0.1355, so i = 97.79°"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"There are six passes each day, three in the evening and three in the morning (UTC), and the mean pass lasts 580.8 s"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"With a 4 m² sail the satellite re-enters about two years after the end of the mission"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusion is specific, quantitative and derived directly from the body (revisit, passes, margin, decay lifetime), and honestly enumerates limitations and next steps. Not generic.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations. First, Vega C has not yet flown, so the Falcon 9 rideshare service is kept as a fallback"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear engineering structure, concise abstract, numbered sections, captioned figures/tables with sources, consistent IEEE-style citations matching the bibliography, and an appendix listing delivered STK files. Minor weaknesses: contents list without page numbers, a couple of weak web references and some unsupported factual claims (reef area), and no explicit page-limit note.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The design is supported by STK simulations and by hand calculations of the orbit, coverage, data budget and orbital decay."},{"where":"Figure 1","quote":"Figure 1: STK 3D view of the ZYZZsat orbit at two times 12 hours apart (generated in STK 11)."},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","quote":"The following STK 11 files are submitted with this report in ZYZZsat_STK.zip."}]}]}
